MOUTH OF WATER

Duration: 25s
Aspect Ratio: 16:9 cinematic
FPS: 24 or 30
Color: Black and White only
Style: sensual noir surrealism / macro art-film intimacy
Anchor Rule: use the original fish-finger image as the primary visual anchor for proximity, contact, framing logic, and tactile mood
Character Rule: only the female character implied by the anchor image; no other human presence
Music Rule: cut and motion cadence must breathe with the provided CWPP cue: seductive unease → intimate pull → dark shimmer → unresolved craving

ABSOLUTE RULE ZERO
NO REPEATED INSERTION RHYTHM
NO COMEDY
NO HORROR ATTACK
NO GORE
NO OVER-ACTING
NO FULL-BODY REVEAL
NO OTHER PEOPLE
NO COLOR

The scene must feel like:
a private ritual of invitation, contact, and suspended desire through water and glass.

HARD TIMING LAW
Total length: 25.0s
Average shot length: 1.2–2.2s
No shot longer than 2.8s
Rhythm should feel breathing-led, not action-led
Cuts should follow:
inhaled pauses
whispered phrases
soft beat arrivals
harmonic sting
unresolved ending
CORE VISUAL IDEA

A woman, seen only in fragments, stands before an aquarium in black-and-white.
Her fingertip approaches a large fish through a narrow tank opening.
The gesture is subtle, coaxing, private, almost ritualistic.
The fish receives the contact with slow, unnerving softness.
The scene unfolds like a confession no one was meant to witness.

CAMERA LAW
Macro / extreme macro only
Shallow depth of field
Very slow push-ins
Focus breathing allowed
Soft rack focus between:
fingertip
black-polished nail
fish eye
fish lip texture
condensation / waterline / reflection
Human face appears only as:
blurred reflection
cheek edge
lips out of focus
silhouette fragment
Camera must feel near enough to be invasive
LIGHTING LAW
Pure black-and-white
High-latitude grayscale, velvety blacks, silver highlights
Wet specular highlights on skin and glass
Water glow should bloom softly
Reflections drift across glass like breath or memory
Contrast should feel luxurious, not harsh
SOUNDTRACK SYNC RULE

Match the cue arc exactly:

0.0–5.0s: reversed texture / inhale / distant pad bloom
5.0–11.0s: sub bass + dry beat enter
11.0–18.0s: hypnotic dark lift
18.0–22.0s: peak intimacy
22.0–25.0s: strip back / unresolved cut

Visual escalation should follow that emotional contour, never over-explaining.

SCENE-BY-SCENE BEATS
0.00–1.80

Shot 1 — already close
Extreme macro of wet aquarium glass in black-and-white.
The fish is already there, just out of perfect focus, mouth and eye partially visible.
A woman’s fingertip enters frame slowly from the edge, hovering before contact.
Tiny suspended particles drift like dust in water.

Purpose: immediate intimacy, no setup, viewer arrives too late.

1.80–3.60

Shot 2 — first invitation
Macro on the fingertip and glossy dark nail.
The finger lightly traces condensation or the lip line of the opening, not entering yet.
The fish barely shifts forward.
The movement is almost nothing — that is the point.

Music sync: reversed texture, faint inhale.

3.60–5.20

Shot 3 — reflection fragment
Soft-focus reflection of the woman’s lower face in the tank glass — cheek, lips, maybe nose line, never fully clear.
Her breathing subtly clouds the surface.
Foreground: the fish eye catches a silver highlight.

Purpose: woman becomes presence, not character exposition.

5.20–7.10

Shot 4 — first contact
With the beat entering, the fingertip finds the narrow opening and barely crosses into the water.
The fish does not lunge. It simply receives the presence.
The water trembles around the skin.

Music sync: sub bass + dry beat begin.

7.10–9.00

Shot 5 — the slight curl
Macro from side angle.
The fingertip makes the smallest inward curl, not repeated, not performative — just enough to feel like invitation.
The fish drifts closer and answers the gesture by closing distance itself.

Important: no repeated “come here” motion. One subtle invitation only.

9.00–11.20

Shot 6 — acceptance
Extreme macro on mouth texture and fingertip.
The fish’s mouth softens gently around the finger with delicate pressure.
The finger trembles once, then stills.
Glass flare blooms softly across frame.

Purpose: bodily tension without explicitness.

11.20–13.20

Shot 7 — eye / skin / water
Rack focus sequence:
fish eye → fingertip skin texture → black nail → water distortion.
The contact remains, almost motionless.
What changes is the viewer’s awareness of texture and closeness.

Music sync: harmony darkens, hypnosis begins.

13.20–15.20

Shot 8 — slight yielding
The finger yields a fraction deeper, but only as a consequence of the fish moving closer.
No push. No drive.
The woman’s hand remains controlled, almost reverent.

Purpose: surrender without act-language.

15.20–17.30

Shot 9 — breath on glass
Cut to glass surface from outside angle.
Her blurred lips near the glass, just out of focus.
A faint bloom of breath fog appears and fades.
Inside the tank, the fish and finger remain joined as a ghostly blur.

Purpose: human desire is present, but displaced into atmosphere.

17.30–19.10

Shot 10 — peak intimacy
Return to the closest macro of contact in the entire piece.
The fish mouth flexes softly.
Tiny ripples pulse around the finger.
The finger does not move except for a minute involuntary tremor.

Music sync: layered whisper ad-libs, peak intimacy.

19.10–20.80

Shot 11 — the near-withdrawal
The finger eases back by a few millimeters, as if reconsidering.
The fish follows slightly, not aggressively, just enough to preserve the bond.
That hesitation creates the most charged beat in the piece.

Purpose: almost losing contact is stronger than increasing it.

20.80–22.20

Shot 12 — suspended hold
Macro stillness.
The finger and fish settle into a fragile equilibrium.
A silver highlight skims across the nail and lower lip of the fish.
Everything feels expensive, forbidden, and quiet.

22.20–23.60

Shot 13 — release begins
As the soundtrack strips back, the finger slowly withdraws from the water opening.
The fish remains near, mouth slightly parted, as if waiting.
A thin thread of water clings to the skin, then breaks.

Music sync: pulse + breath + hanging note.

23.60–25.00

Shot 14 — unresolved ending
Final macro on the fingertip just outside the opening, hovering again, almost returning.
The fish lingers in front of it.
No resolution. No full withdrawal. No final gesture.
Cut on the feeling that the contact might resume one second later.

End state: craving suspended.

